0011  clang: the Microsoft compiler barriers on 32-bit Arm (Windows)

_ReadWriteBarrier, _ReadBarrier and _WriteBarrier were not builtins on ARM32. <intrin.h> declares
all three for every architecture, so a call compiled silently to an EXTERNAL call and failed at
link with an undefined symbol, and `#pragma intrinsic(_ReadWriteBarrier)` warned
-Wignored-pragma-intrinsic, which is an error under -Werror. MSVC's ARM compiler inlines all three,
and clang already does on x86 and AArch64. Chromium's sqlite hits it.

Declared in BuiltinsARM.def exactly as AArch64 declares _ReadWriteBarrier (MS languages, intrin.h,
nothrow, requires declaration), and lowered in EmitARMBuiltinExpr to the same single-thread
sequentially-consistent fence x86 and AArch64 emit: a compiler barrier, no instruction.

Tests: ms-barriers-intrinsics.c, the barriers' own test, gains a thumbv7 RUN line (through
<intrin.h>, as sqlite uses them); arm-microsoft-intrinsics.c checks the no-header call path and
the non-MS negative; Preprocessor/pragma_microsoft_arm.c checks #pragma intrinsic is silent.

Against rt2-src (== llvmorg-23.1.1 + rt2-vs-23.1.1.patch). Generated, not written by hand.

